WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
For eight years, Hugh Laurie starred in the
title role on the FOX medical drama House.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.380
But despite becoming an international celebrity,
he isn't chasing success for its own sake.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.520
Here's why he hasn't been the same since House.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.400
For an actor, not every day on the job is
glamorous and exciting.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.600
Sometimes it can feel a little repetitive.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.180
So after nearly a decade playing Dr. Gregory
House, Hugh Laurie definitely wanted a change

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:22.740
of scenery.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.740
He still enjoyed playing the role all the
way through to the final season, but the routine

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.179
was starting to get monotonous, and he wasn't
sure if he was really cut out for that kind

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.060
of commitment anymore.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.560
As the final season was airing, he told The
Telegraph,

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.700
"You know, we have done 170 episodes now,
I think.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.020
That's about 50-60 feature films-worth.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.880
You want a break, you really do."

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.700
So Laurie's first order of business after
House wrapped was to take a long break from

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.200
acting.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.100
He ended up spending a full three years away
from Hollywood, save for a few small exceptions,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.840
like lending his voice to the video game LittleBigPlanet
3.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.120
He finally got back in front of the camera
in 2015 for the Disney film Tomorrowland.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.780
He loved playing House, but he often felt
like he was working himself to the bone on

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.280
set.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.100
Getting up at 5 AM for a 16-hour workday was
not uncommon, and he stuck with this schedule

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.100
for nearly a decade.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.500
No wonder he needed an extended vacation once
the cameras stopped rolling.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.060
"How are things in hell?

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.680
Is the humidity the big issue?"

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.900
Although Laurie would certainly have preferred
more privacy than the press afforded him after

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.720
the success of House, the overwhelming praise
for the series gave him a major confidence

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.220
boost.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.820
After all, he'd never been a part of such
an internationally successful show before.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.680
Previously, he occasionally felt insecure
about his career because he'd never gone to

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.880
drama school, and he wondered if he wasn't
a genuinely qualified actor.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.520
Laurie used to question whether or not he
really belonged in the entertainment industry.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.500
But once House took off with audiences, and
he saw the impact the show was having, he

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.330
realized that he'd definitely earned his place.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.710
It was a huge turning point in his career.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:45.700
As he told The Guardian,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.160
"House was something I didn't have to apologize
for.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.370
It was something I was really proud of and...whether
you liked it or not, it was undeniable."

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.080
Although Laurie wasn't acting much right after
House went off the air, he didn't completely

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:57.840
stop working.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.119
He just chose to invest his time in a different
creative pursuit.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:05.110
It turns out he's a talented blues musician,
and he loves recording music even more than

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:05.660
he loves acting.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.600
In 2011, Laurie released Let Them Talk, his
first blues album.

00:02:09.609 --> 00:02:13.700
He was surprised by the success it achieved,
and he eventually went on to release a second

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.510
album in 2013, entitled Didn't It Rain.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.540
He admitted that he was quite nervous in the
recording studio, but as it turned out, he

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.820
had nothing to be worried about.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.120
His concerts sold out, and the albums were
certified gold.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.120
He told The Guardian in 2013,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.760
"There's a sensual pleasure involved in making
sounds, harmonious sounds, that I just can't

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.480
get, and I don't think anyone can quite get,
from acting…It just cannot get better than

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:35.980
that.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.960
It takes over my whole body in a way that
acting just can't."

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.020
Dr. Gregory House was known for being prickly
and brutally honest, but it would be tough

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.420
to argue that he was a villain.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.680
Despite his rough exterior, he had a kind
heart, and he genuinely wanted to help his

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.200
patients.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.159
But the actor who played him eventually wanted
to play a truly evil character.

00:02:54.159 --> 00:02:58.620
In 2016, Hugh Laurie finally got that chance
when he landed the role of Richard Roper on

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.700
the BBC series The Night Manager.

00:03:01.029 --> 00:03:05.080
Based on John le Carré's novel of the same
name, the plot follows a former soldier who

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.519
manages to worm his way into Roper's inner
circle as Roper is arming the Egyptian government

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.769
in the midst of the Arab spring.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.100
At one point, Roper is described as "the worst
man in the world," and Laurie was up for the

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.300
challenge of bringing this vile character
to life.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.650
He told The Telegraph,

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.080
"Villainy serves a purpose in all good storytelling.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.150
It's a very well-trodden path, in all kinds
of stories, so when I was offered the chance

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.120
to play [Roper], there was no way I could
possibly pass it up."

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.480
"Her life depends on the quality of your performance
in the next half hour.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.220
So smile."

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.159
After eight years of playing House, Laurie
knew that he could easily land another doctor

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:45.069
role if he wanted to, but he felt like doing
so would mean he was basically putting himself

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:45.560
in a box.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.960
But when he was offered the part of Dr. Eldon
Chance on the Hulu series Chance, he just

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:50.740
couldn't pass it up.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:55.500
When asked in a 2016 interview with Junkee
if he hesitated before taking another medical

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:56.519
role, he answered,

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.519
"For about two minutes.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.820
I opened the script and thought, 'Oh, this
is good.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.900
It's a shame I can't do it because it's medical.'

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.740
After three pages I'd forgotten."

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:07.620
Laurie made an exception this time because
Dr. Chance was a different type of doctor

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.389
who was dealing with very different patients
than Dr. House was.

00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:13.760
"No, I'm not a treating therapist, I'm a consulting
neuropsychiatrist."

00:04:14.129 --> 00:04:17.960
Chance is a medical drama and a suspenseful
thriller all wrapped up in one, and Laurie

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.400
didn't feel like he was playing the same role
all over again.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.460
Overall, he's certainly changed in the years
since the House finale, but the authenticity

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.320
that he brings to every role still makes him
one of the best actors in the business.

